/**
 * sofi palette -- the map of where colour comes from.
 *
 * This file used to be the palette. It is now the file that explains it: the
 * values moved into one file per palette when sofi gained three of them, and
 * the semantic names moved into roles.sasi, because a palette and a role map
 * are different things and keeping them together is exactly why swapping the
 * first silently broke the second.
 *
 * Nothing here defines a colour. The path does not change:
 * /org/sofi/palette.sasi is still compiled in, still carries the sixteen slots
 * and the derived tones, still parsed immediately before whichever built-in
 * panel layout the invocation selected -- it is the same bytes as
 * doc/palette-sakura.sasi, which is the default palette. The layouts still
 * contain no hex at all.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHERE EACH VALUE LIVES NOW
 *
 *   doc/palette-sakura.sasi        violet and dusty rose. The default, and
 *                                  also compiled in as the plain
 *                                  /org/sofi/palette.sasi above.
 *   doc/palette-sakura-dark.sasi   ash black, muted berry and plum.
 *   doc/palette-sakura-light.sasi  pearl white, emerald and slate blue.
 *
 * Each holds sixteen positional slots, that palette's own five derived tones,
 * and the four values a role map cannot derive from a slot index. Exactly one
 * of the three is parsed, selected by `-palette <name>`, defaulting to
 * `sakura`.
 *
 *   doc/roles.sasi                 the semantic names -- what each colour is
 *                                  FOR. Parsed immediately after whichever
 *                                  palette was selected. This is the file the
 *                                  layouts refer to, and the file that records
 *                                  the measured contrast ratio of every role
 *                                  on every palette.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ONE PALETTE, TWO PROGRAMS
 *
 * Each palette's sixteen slots are byte-identical to the block of the same name
 * in hikari-sakura's `ui { palette }`, and the semantic names are mapped onto
 * that compositor's own `ui { colorscheme }` slot by slot:
 *
 *     sofi accent        = hikari `selected`
 *     sofi accent-soft   = color4  = hikari `first`
 *     sofi accent-strong = color13 = hikari `insert`
 *     sofi muted         = color8  = hikari `inactive`
 *     sofi background    = color0 @ 90% = hikari `bar`, exactly
 *
 * That last one is why a sofi panel sitting under the top bar reads as the same
 * surface rather than as a window laid over it. The layout is the conventional
 * terminal one -- 0-7 normal, 8-15 bright -- which is what lets one sixteen
 * value scheme dress the compositor, its bar and this shell at once, and what
 * makes a terminal colorscheme droppable in wholesale.
 *
 * Two of the mappings are no longer positional. `accent` is whichever of
 * color12 and color4 measures better against the palette's own ground, and
 * `urgent` and `critical` are a literal red chosen by that ground's luminance
 * rather than a slot at all. hikari's `selected` is color12, so on the default
 * palette the two still agree exactly; on Sakura Light sofi's accent is color4,
 * because color12 reads at 3.32:1 there and a selected row has to be readable.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE ONE PLACE THIS TREE HOLDS A COLOUR THAT NO SLOT DETERMINES
 *
 * This file used to say it was the only file in this tree that contains a
 * colour value. That is no longer true and the reason is worth stating rather
 * than leaving as a stale promise.
 *
 * `urgent` and `critical` are two literal reds held outside every palette,
 * selected by the ground's relative luminance: #df8787 on a dark ground and
 * #9b2226 on a light one. Per R-103 the window for a single value is empty, not
 * merely tight -- clearing AA against the darkest ground this desktop ships
 * requires a luminance at or above 0.21147, and clearing it against the
 * lightest requires 0.11530 or below, and those do not intersect. So one red is
 * a choice of which palette to fail on. #df8787 is byte-identical to the
 * default palette's color9, which is what `urgent` resolved to before the
 * split, so the default renders exactly as it did.
 *
 * Both stay overridable by name. Everything else in the tree is still either a
 * slot, a tone derived from one, or a reference to one.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * OVERRIDING IT
 *
 * `@` references resolve at property lookup, not at parse, and every parse has
 * finished before the first widget is built. So a `* { }` block in
 * ~/.config/sofi/config.sasi is parsed after this file and still wins -- for
 * every surface at once. Redefining `color12` recolours all four panels;
 * redefining `accent` recolours their selections without touching anything else
 * that happens to use color12. The split changes nothing about that: the
 * palette is parsed first, roles.sasi second, your config last, and a role that
 * reaches its palette through a `palette-` name is still a chain of links that
 * resolves after your block has been read.
 *
 * The one thing to know is which slot a role resolves to on the palette you
 * are using, because that is now measured rather than fixed. roles.sasi records
 * it. On Sakura Light, redefining `color12` does not move the selection --
 * `color4` does, because that is what the accent measured to there.
 *
 * See sofi-customisation(5).
 */
